# Verify

> Last stage of the feature flow. A reviewer that never saw the build conversation checks the
> branch against the spec; QA runs in three layers, cheapest first; the PR maps every R# to the
> test that proves it.
>
> **Usage:** `/battoni-verify <id>`
> Reference: `.claude/feature-flow.md`. Reads `intent.md`, `spec.md`, `plan.md` → `## QA`, `state.md` and the branch diff only —
> **never `proposal.html`** (the Chrome driver opens it, not you).

## Step 0 — Preconditions

- `state.md` says every phase is done, with `pre-commit green` on the Done line. Otherwise stop
  and say which phase is missing.
- Set `state.md` to `Stage: verify`.
- Note the suite counts from `state.md`; they go into the PR.

## Step 1 — Review against the spec (Fable finds, you check)

Run it **once**, on the whole branch, before any Chrome round: a finding fixed after QA means QA
runs again. Spawn the `fable-reviewer` agent (user level, `~/.claude/agents/`) with: the path of `spec.md`,
the base (`main`), the sub-projects touched and the rule files that apply to them
(by path — it reads only the ones named), picked by each sub-project's Type as in step 2 of
`.claude/agents/code-reviewer.md`: always `shared-conventions.md`, the `.claude/rules/` files for
an API or an app, and for anything else the row's own `CLAUDE.md` or `README.md` plus the
`RULES.md` chain of the changed files. It checks the spec **and** those
conventions: every R# implemented, every A# with a test that asserts it, nothing built that the
spec does not ask for. Do not send it the conversation or your opinion of the build.

Never a `general-purpose` agent on Fable: it starts at ~69k tokens of context against ~13k for
`fable-reviewer` (measured 2026-11-03; one such review cost US$ 3.62). Fable needs the owner's
permission (or `#fable` in the session; an away run uses it only when its session had `#fable`
and `## Away run` says `Reviewer: fable-reviewer`); without it, or when `fable-reviewer` is not installed,
spawn `code-reviewer` on Sonnet with the same inputs.

Check each finding in the file before accepting it. Real → fix (red test first when it is
behaviour), `/battoni-pre-commit`, commit. Rejected → one line with the reason; it goes into the PR.

## Step 2 — QA, three layers, one script

Each layer starts only when the one before passed. Layers 2 and 3 run **the same script**, so a
step fixed in Chrome is fixed for the person too.

1. **Tests** — green from the build (every A# has its test), under the phase gate. Run the full
   `/battoni-pre-commit` once on the final branch: the feature's only run of the full app
   Playwright suite (`.claude/feature-flow.md`, Phase gate).
2. **The script** — spawn one `general-purpose` agent with `model: sonnet`. Give it the paths of
   `spec.md`, `plan.md` (its `## QA` block), the `battoni-test-in-chrome` skill (Passo 1, its
   format) and the app's locale files; it writes `specs/<id>-*/qa-script.md`. One round per R#;
   the round opens with its seed command (`php artisan db:seed --class=<Feature>QaSeeder`, from
   `## QA` → Data) and its QA login link; each step is action → exact pt-BR label on screen → the
   A# it checks. An A# already proved at a width by Playwright is marked `Chrome: skip (<test>)`
   at that width; the person still gets the step. **Only invented records**: the ones the QA and
   demo seeders create, never a value from a database pulled from production. Commit it.
3. **Chrome driver** — load the `battoni-test-in-chrome` skill and run `qa-script.md` as its
   script (it skips Passo 1). Every round signs in by opening the API's QA login link,
   `/api/qa/login-as/<username>?to=/<route>`, on the API host (local only — `QaLoginAsController`),
   and never reuses the session of the round before. When the spec header says `(approved …)`, add
   the round **Compare with the approved demo** described in the skill, with the `## Screens` list
   of the spec. You judge only the failures and the differences; a step that was wrong is fixed
   **in `qa-script.md`**, so the person never meets it. **The rounds run on the data the person
   will have:** when `## QA` → Human round says yes, a separate local database built only by
   `migrate:fresh --seed` and the QA seeder (`DB_DATABASE=<project>_qa<n>`), never a production
   pull. `qa-script.md` → `## Resultados` opens with the database the rounds ran on.
4. **A person (a tester, the client)** — when `## QA` → Human round says yes. Spawn one
   `general-purpose` agent with `model: sonnet` that loads `battoni-new-qa-session` and turns
   `qa-script.md` into the Claude Docs doc, one section per R#, each step citing its A#, then the
   free-use section: the steps and labels are already checked, so it reads no code and no i18n.
   That holds only when `## Resultados` names a seeders-only database; when the rounds ran on
   other data, the skill's data inventory and Chrome pre-check run before the doc is shared.
   It returns only the link; the doc is private until the owner shares it. When `## QA` says
   "merge waits", the PR stays a draft and `state.md` → Next is "<tester> runs the doc, then
   `/battoni-qa-result <link>`". The doc's link goes into `state.md` → `## QA` the moment it
   exists (`Layer 3 (human, <tester>, <env>): pending. <title> <link>`): it is how
   `/battoni-qa-result` finds this feature days later, merged or not.
   *Retired 10/11/2026: "layer 3 only when the owner asks".* In boanerges-3 nobody asked until
   after the merge, so the script was written last, on Opus at 140–209k of context (US$ 4.70 with
   its unplanned seeder), and it could no longer gate the merge.

Each layer's result is one line in `state.md` → `## QA`.

**Away mode** (`/battoni-run`): the script and the human doc are written as above; the person's
round itself is owed, in the PR. With `Chrome QA: off` in `## Away run`, the Chrome rounds do not
run: `## QA` and the PR list each one as owed, and the human doc is not written, since its steps
were never checked. Nobody answers questions: see the last bullet below.

Problems found:
- **violates an A#** → a bug: a failing test, then the fix (in this branch while it is open; after
  merge, the small track with its own ID, citing the R#);
- **outside the spec** → either the script was wrong (fix the script) or it is a new wish: a new
  D# for the owner, or a new intent. Never fix silently. Away, it is a `W#` and a line in the PR
  for the owner, never built.

## Step 3 — PR

**Cost.** When `plans/tokens/scripts/branch.py` exists (it lives in the private `plans/`), run
`python3 plans/tokens/scripts/branch.py | grep 'feature/<id>'` and write
`Cost: US$ <x> (main <y>, subagents <z>) · <date>` under `## QA`; without it, `Cost: not measured`.
It counts every session on the branch, intent to verify.

**Clean tree.** `git status --porcelain` in the feature worktree must be empty, untracked files
included. Anything there is either committed now or deleted with a reason; nothing reaches `main`
after the merge outside a PR (`.claude/feature-flow.md`, "After delivery").

Load `/battoni-create-pr`. Title `<id>: <imperative subject>`. The body opens with the intent's
problem and outcome in two lines and a table **R# → test file::test name**, then the review
findings not applied with the reason, the QA lines (the human doc's link, and whether the merge
waits for it), the cost line, and the suite counts. As the branch's last commit, set `state.md` to
`Stage: delivered — PR #<n>` and Next to "merge" (or to the human round, when the merge waits):
once merged, it is true. Every `state.md` line verify wrote and did not commit rides in this one.

End with: the PR link, layers passed, anything that could not be tested and why, and when there is
a human doc, its link and "share it with <tester> with edit permission".
